<div class="wrap">
  <h1>Take OrcaSlicer's message where its reason is still known</h1>
  <div class="status"><p><strong>Second version, rewritten after your review. Still a proposal:</strong> you have agreed to none of it, and to no route in the inventory. This time there is a prototype. It is uncommitted in the worktree <code>youthful-khorana-e9acfc</code>, on <code>jusprin-dev</code> at <code>cccef9f228</code>, and saved as a patch. Thirteen cases were run through OrcaSlicer's real code on macOS, each once with the prototype off and once with it on. Nothing was committed or pushed.</p></div>
  <ul class="toc">
    <li><a href="#review">What your review changed</a></li>
    <li><a href="#seam">The seam</a></li>
    <li><a href="#ran">What was run</a></li>
    <li><a href="#cost">What it costs in OrcaSlicer's files</a></li>
    <li><a href="#limits">What it does not do</a></li>
    <li><a href="#held">The two held commits</a></li>
    <li><a href="#decisions">Decisions needed</a></li>
    <li><a href="#rows">All 306 rows</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="review">What your review changed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The first version judged the earlier attempts wrongly.</strong> They started at the source because that is where the reason is: which printer, which command, who asked. When a dialog opens, that reason is gone. What they lacked was a list of the sources and a check that fails when one is missed.</li>
    <li><strong>“JusPrin says it” had only OrcaSlicer's words.</strong> It could not put a message on the right printer's card.</li>
    <li><strong>It claimed to need no line in OrcaSlicer's files.</strong> Keeping the printer needs lines there. This version needs three, and one changed line that is already JusPrin's.</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing had been run.</strong> The sorting rule, the save-then-answer step and the page guard were all argued from reading.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="seam">The seam</h2>
  <p>Take one case. A Bambu printer answers a print job with FAIL. OrcaSlicer's printer code knows which printer said so, and calls <code>GUI_App::push_notification(printer, "Failed to start print job")</code>. That function puts an event in the queue and returns. A moment later one handler opens a box titled Info, holding the text and nothing else. The printer was last known inside <code>push_notification</code>.</p>
  <p>OrcaSlicer tells the person something in one of two ways, and the reason survives differently in each.</p>
  <div class="piece">
    <h3>At once</h3>
    <p>OrcaSlicer opens the box in the same call that decided to speak. The code that asked for the work is still running underneath it. So a note JusPrin made about what is being handled is still true at the moment the box opens, and the hook JusPrin already has on every modal box can read it there.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="piece">
    <h3>Later</h3>
    <p>Three functions put the message off and return: <code>show_error</code> with about 138 callers, <code>GUI_App::show_dialog</code> with 5, and <code>GUI_App::push_notification</code> with 7. By the time the box opens, the caller is gone. One line in each function hands JusPrin the message while the caller is still there, together with what the function was given: the printer, or the window the message belongs to.</p>
  </div>
  <p>The note about what is being handled I call the <strong>cause</strong>: a kind, a printer, and a detail such as the plug-in's code. JusPrin sets it at the few doors where work comes in. Most of those doors already have JusPrin code standing at them.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>What comes in</th><th>Who notes the cause</th><th>New line in OrcaSlicer's file</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A printer answers a connection</td><td>JusPrin's existing line in the connect handler (<code>record_connection_event</code>)</td><td>None</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A printer sends a status or a reply</td><td><code>push_notification</code> is given the printer</td><td>One, the hand-over line</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The plug-in sends the app a message</td><td><code>process_network_msg</code> is given the plug-in's code</td><td>One</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A slice ends in an error</td><td>JusPrin's existing listener for the end of a slice, which runs before OrcaSlicer's own</td><td>None</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An upload to a print host fails</td><td>JusPrin's existing listener on the upload queue</td><td>None</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The AI runs a tool, or a file is opened</td><td>The existing listeners, unchanged</td><td>None</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The person uses OrcaSlicer's own menu or a key</td><td>Nobody. The message has no cause.</td><td>None</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The third line is in <code>show_dialog</code>. It has no cause of its own to give, but it is where a queued message would otherwise lose the cause that the connect handler noted a few lines earlier.</p>

  <h3>What happens to a message once it is taken</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It names a printer.</strong> It belongs on that printer: its card on Home, its chat, the header. That part is not built. The run shows the printer's id arriving with the words.</li>
    <li><strong>It is about a slice or an upload.</strong> JusPrin already shows both failures in its own places, so OrcaSlicer's box was a second copy. It is dropped.</li>
    <li><strong>It has no cause.</strong> It is about the project on screen. In the prototype it becomes a short confirmation on the canvas, the place where OrcaSlicer's own notices about an action's outcome have gone since 12 October.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>What still opens as OrcaSlicer's own</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A file or folder picker, including during a file open, where today it is cancelled before it draws.</li>
    <li>A question with a real choice.</li>
    <li>A window with fields, such as the send dialog.</li>
    <li>Any box while another window is open over the main one. You are looking at that window, so the box belongs with it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Two more things use the same hook and the same idea. “Transfer or discard changes” is recognised by its class: a version is saved, the edits are dropped, and the dialog is answered. And a switch of the main window to a page JusPrin hides is refused by a listener on the page control. OrcaSlicer's own Ctrl+Tab code already expects a page to refuse: its comment reads “Skip a tab that vetoes the switch”.</p>
  <h3>What a miss looks like now</h3>
  <p>A message from a source nobody listed still does not open as an OrcaSlicer box. It is taken with no cause and shows as a plain confirmation. The prototype keeps two lists while it runs: every message it took, with its cause, and every window it let through, with its class and the reason. A check built on those lists can fail a harness run when a message that came from a printer or the plug-in has no cause, or when a window's class is not on the list. That check is not built.</p>

