  <p>The pill checks fail now and then without the prototype too, so I do not count them against it. I ran the readers mode only once without the prototype, so I cannot say the same of its one failure. Across those modes the prototype took three messages: the two failed connections in the printer setup mode, with their printer, and the slice error. It let through JusPrin's own Check print and Preferences windows and two of OrcaSlicer's questions.</p>

  <h2 id="cost">What it costs in OrcaSlicer's files</h2>
  <p>Three new lines in <code>GUI_App.cpp</code>, one each at the top of <code>show_dialog</code>, <code>push_notification</code> and <code>process_network_msg</code>. One changed line in <code>GUI.cpp</code>, in <code>show_error</code>, which is already JusPrin's own line from the file-load work. Everything else is in JusPrin's files. I simulated taking OrcaSlicer's newest commit, <code>07f38df2a4</code> of 18 November, over 2.5.0. The result has the same four conflicted files with the prototype and without it, and none of them is <code>GUI.cpp</code> or <code>GUI_App.cpp</code>.</p>
  <p>For comparison, the second held commit edits four OrcaSlicer lines to silence three texts from one handler. These three lines and the changed one cover every message through all three functions, about 150 callers today.</p>

  <h2 id="limits">What it does not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The printer error popup with action buttons.</strong> OrcaSlicer opens it without the modal call, so the hook never sees it, and its buttons cannot be offered anywhere else. The prototype leaves it as it is. I have no answer for it.</li>
    <li><strong>The place where a printer's message appears.</strong> The run proves the printer's id arrives with the words. Putting them on the card, the chat or the header is not built and needs a board.</li>
    <li><strong>A plug-in message names no printer.</strong> The run showed that OrcaSlicer handles these messages only when the device id is empty. The plug-in's code arrives; a printer does not.</li>
    <li><strong>About 19 places where OrcaSlicer defers a box by itself</strong>, without any of the three functions. Those reach the hook with no cause and become plain messages. The count is from a search, not from reading each one.</li>
    <li><strong>JusPrin's own plain messages.</strong> A JusPrin box with only OK on the main window is taken by the same rule unless JusPrin marks it as its own. Three of JusPrin's questions are marked today. None of the others came up in the eight existing modes.</li>
    <li><strong>The dialog that copies one nozzle's settings to the other.</strong> It uses the same class as “Transfer or discard changes”. The prototype tells them apart by the title, looked up the way OrcaSlicer looks it up. That was not run, because it needs a two-nozzle printer in the fixture.</li>
    <li><strong>The Device page itself.</strong> Without the Bambu plug-in it is not in the page list, so the harness could not enter it even with the prototype off. The guard was shown on Calibration and Preview.</li>
    <li><strong>The list of keys</strong>, such as Ctrl+Shift+G opening OrcaSlicer's print dialog. Not built.</li>
    <li><strong>A message with only OK that is followed by code relying on it.</strong> The code after such a box gets the same answer whether or not anyone read it, so taking the box cannot change what the code does. What can be lost is the reading, when the app quits or restarts right after. I did not go through the callers for that.</li>
    <li><strong>The census of sources and the failing check.</strong> Not built.</li>
    <li><strong>Windows and Linux.</strong> Everything ran on macOS, in a Release build.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="held">What happens to the two held commits</h2>
  <p><strong>The connect popup</strong> (<code>64f9168a21</code>). The prototype removes the popup without touching the three places that produce the text, and it keeps what the held commit throws away: the printer, the code and OrcaSlicer's hints. Its four edited OrcaSlicer lines would not land. One existing check would need to flip: the printer setup mode expects the “Incorrect password” box to show.</p>
  <p><strong>The header printer switch</strong> (<code>311a313801</code>). The prototype answers the dialog where it opens. The run covers the header's path and the import window's path, which the held commit does not reach. Its five inserted calls would not be needed. I did not check whether the activity list still shows one switch, which that commit's transaction lines are there for.</p>

  <h2 id="decisions">Decisions needed</h2>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>Whether to build on this seam</h3>
    <p><span class="lead">Decision needed.</span> Should JusPrin take OrcaSlicer's messages at the three functions that put a message off and at the modal hook, with three new lines in OrcaSlicer's files? This is yours because it adds lines the fork carries at every OrcaSlicer release.</p>
    <p>The three lines buy the printer for every printer message and the cause across the delay, for about 150 callers at once. The other way to keep the reason is the held commit's: an edit at each place that produces a text, which is four lines for three texts in one handler. A version with no line at all is what I first proposed, and it loses the printer.</p>
    <p>My pick is to build on it. Do you agree?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>Where a taken message is shown</h3>
    <p><span class="lead">Decision needed.</span> Where should you see a message once JusPrin has taken it? This is yours because it is new screen design and needs a board before code.</p>
    <p>For a message with no cause, the prototype uses the confirmation on the canvas that OrcaSlicer's own notices already use. It costs nothing new, but it exists only on the project screen. For a message that names a printer, the choices are that printer's card on Home, its chat, the header's printer menu, or all three. JusPrin already reads the printer's connection state in each of them, so the words would join a state that is already shown.</p>
    <p>My pick is the canvas confirmation for messages with no cause, and the printer's card and chat for messages that name a printer. Which do you want?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>What happens to unsaved setting changes on a switch</h3>
    <p><span class="lead">Decision needed.</span> When you switch printer or filament with unsaved setting changes, should JusPrin save a version and then drop those changes, or save a version and carry them over where OrcaSlicer offers Transfer? This is yours because it decides what happens to someone's edits.</p>
    <p>Dropping is what the AI's tools and the printer panel have done since 14 October, what the held commit does, and what the prototype was run with. Its cost is that a tweak made before a switch is gone until you restore the version. Carrying over keeps the tweak but applies a value chosen for one printer or filament to another without telling you, and it was not run.</p>
    <p>My pick is to drop. Is that right?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>What a refused page switch does instead</h3>
    <p><span class="lead">Decision needed.</span> When something tries to switch the main window to OrcaSlicer's Device page, should JusPrin open its own monitor window for that printer, or do nothing? This is yours because, by the inventory's reading, a Bambu send ends with that switch, so it decides what you see right after sending a print.</p>
    <p>The prototype only refuses the switch. Opening the monitor window is more work and gives you a sign that the print was sent. Doing nothing leaves you on Prepare with no sign.</p>
    <p>My pick is to open the monitor window. Do you agree?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>The two held commits until this is built</h3>
    <p><span class="lead">Decision needed.</span> Should both held commits stay unpushed until this is built? This is yours because you stopped the case-by-case fixes.</p>
    <p>If they stay held, released builds keep showing both dialogs until the first piece of this lands. If the header one lands now, that dialog goes sooner, in JusPrin's files only, and it settles the drop-or-carry-over question before you answer it. The connect popup commit would not land in either case, because the prototype does more with fewer OrcaSlicer lines.</p>
    <p>My pick is to keep both held. Which do you want?</p>
  </div>

